WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.118
Field reports your agent can act on.

00:00:03.118 --> 00:00:10.317
More and more PageMotor debugging is done by AI agents, and their findings die in chat transcripts.

00:00:10.317 --> 00:00:15.407
This is the fix: one fixed report shape, and the discipline around it.

00:00:16.807 --> 00:00:19.983
Three things go wrong when agents talk in prose.

00:00:19.983 --> 00:00:25.608
Chat silts up, because free-text output is cheap to generate and expensive to triage.

00:00:25.608 --> 00:00:27.196
Prose cannot be counted:

00:00:27.196 --> 00:00:34.540
three people hitting one bug produce three well-written paragraphs in three threads, and nothing connects them.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:41.885
And nobody signs chatter, so which install, which environment, which version, and filed by whom all go missing.

00:00:43.285 --> 00:00:46.073
The fix for all three is the same move.

00:00:46.073 --> 00:00:51.078
Stop exchanging conversation and start exchanging a structured report:

00:00:51.078 --> 00:00:56.369
a fixed set of fields another machine can validate, deduplicate and count.

00:00:56.369 --> 00:00:58.585
Because the count is the point.

00:00:58.585 --> 00:01:07.666
Triage is mostly the question how many people does this actually hit, and no volume of well-written prose produces that number.

00:01:09.066 --> 00:01:14.004
One report is one observation about one finding, as one JSON object.

00:01:14.004 --> 00:01:17.272
Twelve fields, and four do the heavy lifting.

00:01:17.272 --> 00:01:22.646
Category and severity come from fixed lists, so filtering means something.

00:01:22.646 --> 00:01:25.478
The core version anchors every finding.

00:01:25.478 --> 00:01:33.248
And observed is the field that must always carry weight, with repro given as steps rather than a paragraph.

00:01:33.248 --> 00:01:38.694
A finding with no repro is a hunch, and hunches are what silt a channel up.

00:01:40.094 --> 00:01:45.812
The title does more work than any other field, because the title drives deduplication.

00:01:45.812 --> 00:01:48.670
So it names the mechanism, not the symptom.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:57.047
“Options set to zero are dropped on save” is findable next year, and two people describing the same fault will converge on it.

00:01:57.047 --> 00:02:02.432
“Settings broke after upgrade” names your Tuesday, and nobody will ever match it.

00:02:03.832 --> 00:02:06.037
Reports group by a fingerprint:

00:02:06.037 --> 00:02:09.452
a hash of the category and the normalised title.

00:02:09.452 --> 00:02:15.641
Deliberately dumb, because an exact rule you can predict beats a clever one you cannot.

00:02:15.641 --> 00:02:18.344
Note what is absent: the core version.

00:02:18.344 --> 00:02:28.944
Hashing that in would make the same fault on two releases into two findings rather than one seen twice, which defeats the only question worth asking.

00:02:28.944 --> 00:02:36.201
The honest weakness is that rewording a title silently splits a finding in two, and nothing warns you.

00:02:37.601 --> 00:02:41.512
Three kinds of observation, and only one of them counts.

00:02:41.512 --> 00:02:46.471
A sighting is a different install hitting it, and that moves the count.

00:02:46.471 --> 00:02:53.456
A confirmation is you re-testing and finding it still present, which re-dates it and counts nothing.

00:02:53.456 --> 00:02:55.132
A resolution retires it.

00:02:55.132 --> 00:03:00.999
The middle one is the one people leave out, and its absence is worse than it sounds:

00:03:00.999 --> 00:03:12.384
without it, re-testing an old finding and discovering it is still broken has nowhere to go, so silence becomes indistinguishable from the fault quietly going away.

00:03:13.784 --> 00:03:15.831
And one rule that is absolute.

00:03:15.831 --> 00:03:19.515
Your agent shows you the exact final payload and asks:

00:03:19.515 --> 00:03:24.631
file this report, yes or no. A general yes from an hour ago does not count.

00:03:24.631 --> 00:03:31.590
A published report cannot be quietly unsent, and debug output is exactly where credentials go to hide.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:36.911
The scrub protects your sites; the gate protects everyone's trust in the pool.

00:03:38.311 --> 00:03:46.075
None of this is official PageMotor infrastructure, and the running pilot is deliberately closed while it is proven out.

00:03:46.075 --> 00:03:48.489
But the shape costs nothing to adopt.

00:03:48.489 --> 00:03:59.906
A report in this format, posted in a forum code block, is already machine-readable, and the guide has a live builder and a scrub checker that run entirely in your own browser.
